---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('marsello')!;
const path = '/apps/marsello';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/marsello');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/marsello');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Marsello support Shopify POS and online loyalty?",
    "answer": "Marsello markets loyalty across POS and ecommerce. Demonstrate customer matching, earn, redeem and refund behavior for your connected locations before rollout."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Yotpo loyalty plus email an alternative?",
    "answer": "Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty are separate from retired Yotpo Email and SMS. Evaluate loyalty on its own and select a supported sender; the retired email module is not an active consolidation option."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a quote include?",
    "answer": "Include loyalty plan, every site connection, required tier and segmentation features, marketing options and channel usage in the appropriate currency. Confirm account availability with the supplier."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I use email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Marsello lists both channels in its product range. Confirm plan, region, sender setup, available triggers and channel-specific permission requirements for your account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need another ESP?",
    "answer": "Only a demonstrated requirement should justify another data path. Test the desired audience, journey and suppression behavior before adding or replacing a sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should refunds affect loyalty?",
    "answer": "Define the policy first, then test partial and full refunds, redeemed rewards and tier changes. Reconcile expected and actual ledger entries with support before launch."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the examples measured results?",
    "answer": "No. The staff fixtures and contribution inputs are proposed exercises and hypothetical economics, not performed merchant tests or predicted lifts."
  },
  {
    "question": "Who should consider Marsello?",
    "answer": "Retailers with an intentional rewards program and a need to coordinate store and online loyalty should evaluate it. Existing stack performance and operating economics matter more than a universal order threshold."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Evaluate a shared loyalty operation across Shopify POS and online sales: points, rewards and customer communications need to tell the same story. This review offers proposed acceptance fixtures and economics, not merchant test results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Current scope: loyalty with marketing options</h2>
      <p>Marsello offers loyalty for connected POS and ecommerce operations, with email, SMS and marketing automation in its product range. Its <a href="https://www.marsello.com/">product overview</a> describes the cross-channel purpose. That makes it worth evaluating when staff and customers need the same reward story online and at the counter. It does not establish that every feature or channel is enabled for your account.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.marsello.com/pricing">pricing matrix</a> separates loyalty plans, site connections and marketing options. Tier, expiry, segmentation and product-sync requirements need a plan-level check. Request a quote in the right currency for all locations and expected usage; this review does not reproduce an entry price or rank.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Yotpo comparison: separate loyalty from retired messaging</h2>
      <p>Yotpo scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025 in its <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration notice</a>. Its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">service notice</a> confirms the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Reviews and Loyalty are separate decisions. Existing loyalty use does not supply an active Yotpo email module.</p>
      <p>Compare Marsello against retaining a working loyalty program plus a supported sender. Inventory the fields that sender actually receives, their update delay, who resolves discrepancies and the recurring integration effort. Consolidation is valuable only if it removes work while preserving the required customer and reward records.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Program design before installation</h2>
      <p>Write an earn-and-redeem policy before configuring messages: eligible spend, tax and shipping treatment, excluded products, rounding, reward expiry, referral qualification and staff exceptions. Assign a policy version and an owner for customer disputes. These are proposed preparation tasks, not claims about default Marsello rules.</p>
      <p>Decide whether a tier follows lifetime spend, a rolling period or another documented measure. Specify what a refund should do to earned points and tier status. Marketing and store staff need the same explanation; a polished campaign cannot resolve contradictory counter policies.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Proposed ledger fixture: purchase, refund and redemption</h2>
      <p>Use a staff customer with zero opening points. Under an illustrative policy of one point per eligible dollar, a $60 eligible purchase should produce 60 points. A $20 eligible partial refund should leave 40. Replay the same order update and verify it does not award another 60. Record source order ID, customer ID, policy version and the resulting ledger entries.</p>
      <p>Next rehearse redemption and a later return. Agree whether the reward is reinstated, reduced or consumed, and compare the actual ledger to that policy. Do not silently adjust balances to make the test pass. A mismatch needs an explanation and a repeatable correction procedure before customer-facing balance emails are enabled.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Proposed tier and referral acceptance</h2>
      <p>Put two staff customers immediately below and above the chosen tier boundary. Test a qualifying purchase, a partial refund crossing back below it and a delayed event. Check the displayed tier and the audience selected for a tier message. Set an acceptable refresh delay before testing rather than promising instantaneous updates.</p>
      <p>For referrals, distinguish invitation, first qualifying order and rewarded advocate. Rehearse a cancelled order and repeat referral event. Acceptance means the agreed reward is issued once under the policy and support can trace why. These fixtures do not assert that any particular event is an available automation trigger.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Cross-store identity and channel permission</h2>
      <p>At one POS location, enroll a staff profile and then purchase online using the intended matching identity. Repeat with an alternate email address and an existing duplicate record. Record which profile receives points and whether any merge requires intervention. Expand to another location only after reconciling the first.</p>
      <p>Membership, email marketing permission and SMS permission are separate records. A customer may earn rewards while declining promotional messages. Test that case, an unsubscribe and a changed phone number. Confirm the applicable sender checks current channel permission and suppression; recognition across stores is not permission to contact someone in every channel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Campaign and automation evaluation</h2>
      <p>A useful loyalty campaign could explain a reward balance, invite eligible members to a store event or present a tier benefit. Preview with zero balance, near-redemption balance and missing fields. Make the fallback useful and verify the destination accepts the advertised reward under the same rules.</p>
      <p>For a proposed points reminder or tier journey, demonstrate the actual trigger and data fields in the selected plan. Purchase before a delayed message, change permission while queued and replay an event. Assign one sender and queue owner to each journey and cohort. Do not add another ESP until a demonstrated requirement justifies the extra data path.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Illustrative contribution and reward exposure</h2>
      <p>Assume 20 genuinely incremental repeat orders, $50 order value and 40% contribution before rewards and program costs: 20 × $50 × 0.40 = $400. Subtract assumed $100 redeemed reward cost, $150 platform and messaging cost and $80 operating labor: $70 remains. These are hypothetical inputs, not Marsello prices or measured merchant lift. At zero incremental orders those same costs produce a $330 loss.</p>
      <p>Keep outstanding reward exposure separate from this period contribution calculation. For example, 10,000 redeemable points at an assumed $0.05 fulfillment cost per point represents $500 potential cost if all redeem. Finance should choose the appropriate valuation and treatment; do not assume a universal breakage rate or treat every outstanding point as an immediate expense.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Pilot and release gates</h2>
      <p>Start with a bounded staff exercise, then a small customer cohort only after ledger, permission and reward terms reconcile. Compare repeat behavior over a period suited to the actual repurchase cycle, with comparable cohorts and reward costs included. A fixed ninety-day promise or a universal repeat-purchase target would hide category differences.</p>
      <p>Pause new journey enrollment if balances or reward terms are wrong. Identify pending sends and cancel or drain them before restarting. Preserve ledger evidence and customer corrections. Returning to an earlier configuration must not re-award points or reactivate retired journeys.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Operating cadence and handoff</h2>
      <p>Have store staff log disputed points and identity matches; review unresolved ledger differences before launching another promotion. Marketing should check reward links, fallbacks and audience changes before each send. Finance should reconcile redemptions and outstanding balances on its own agreed schedule.</p>
      <p>The handoff should include location mapping, policy versions, staff exception permissions, refund cases, sender ownership and the support escalation route. A new operator should be able to explain a disputed balance from records rather than from a campaign screenshot.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Who benefits and when to retain the existing stack</h2>
      <p>Retailers running an intentional rewards program across physical and online sales have a concrete reason to trial Marsello. Shared loyalty data can reduce manual coordination between the counter and marketing. An online-only retailer can also evaluate loyalty if the reward proposition is useful; physical stores are not a mandatory qualification.</p>
      <p>If the current loyalty program and sender satisfy the fixtures, retain them unless the proposed change improves operating cost or capability enough to cover migration effort. There is no order-count rule requiring Sequenzy, Klaviyo or another sender. Compare the exact workflow and data transfer rather than claiming universal segmentation superiority.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Related decisions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo retirement and separate loyalty decision</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases/loyalty">Loyalty workflow guide</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Marsello Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
